 Charles000@aol.com wrote: 
>
>Have you had a look at Romana Machado's excellent encrypting
>technique (as per her Stego software) for embedding numerical
>data into the pixel data sets of a bit map image???  Maybe this is
>a bit far fetched, but I like her ideas about being able to "mask"
>hidden information "inside" a bit map file, to the extent that this
>could be a stealth signature embedded directly into the image.

Couldn't this be defeated by the forger randomizing all the
least significant bits and then putting his or her own signature
into them? Or possibly (since the randomizing might be detectable)
not signing it and claiming that he or she thought it was public
domain?

Nancy Lebovitz

Yes Nancy, I fully acknowledge there are any number of ways to alter the
LSB contents to fullfill the goals of either destructive alteration of
the embedded signature, or, in a more sinister sense, as you have correctly
pointed out, the re-implantation of their own signature.  

As others have pointed out as well, there will always be a race between the 
clone/theft slimebags who want to hack a "workaround" to defeat some sort of 
protection scheme (as per the early days of software locks on application 
software), and those who invent the latest protection modalities.

However, this is excactly the type of debate I wanted to instigate, and also,
 I was
suggesting that at least at this point in time, this might be a "temporary",
and
reasonably esoteric way to use Romana's software for this sort of
application.

It may well be, however, that some other process may be a bit more
challenging
(I know, there'e an obvious pun there) to defeat.  I tend to have an interest
in
fractal encoding of imagery (as per Michael Barnsley's fine examples of this
sort
of work), because in this realm, at least, there is no "single" bit values to
play
around with, but rather a "formulation stream" that represent the imagery
content.

In fact, due to the very nature of fractal encryption/compression, the
"factalized"
image data results can be processed as content addressable memory, where the 
feature signatures of the image data are themselves unique, and significant 
reference points (there has been much written about this topic, incidently,
and
the U.S. Air Force, amongst various other interested parties, has spent much
effort
in using this exact modality to enable automatic feature recongnition from
surveillance data, both in optical and near infra-red spectra).

When a "fractalized" image is re-constituted, there could indeed be various
ways
to embed "signature threads" into that reconstruction process.  Perhaps a
"key" to
the signature thread could then be stored, as an "upper layer" of recognition

access, in the LSB of the reconstituted image.  OK, I know, this is just
another 
pandora's box for forger/hacker types to mess around with, but I certainly
welcome, and respect any commentary you wish to toss into the mix on this
topic.

Sincerly
Charles 

 


